From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 06:37:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: g16: Optionally bound how long a ready job
 waits

The engine orders its pending jobs by three keys: the higher priority,
then the queue served least recently, then submission order. The fair
turns between VMs go only to the job it picked, so a low-priority
client beside a higher-priority one that always has a job pending is
never picked. On 1.1088, and on 1.1091 with one compute queue and with
two, a stress test's low-priority compute queue beside a compute client
64 deep got no completion for 15 s on every seed.

The starvation needs no such client. gpubench-stress-vk queue.depth
runs its mixed-priority phase, with low-, medium- and high-priority
devices, from 40 % of its run on, and fails when a queue completes
nothing for 15 s. The release stress run gives the test 8.0 s, so that
phase lasts about 4.8 s and cannot reach the limit. Run alone for 60 s,
the phase begins at 24 s and the limit falls at 39 s: the run failed
at 39.1 s in each of five runs on 1.1115 (seeds 0, 1, 2, 3 and 0) and
at 39 s in each of five runs on 1.1117.

Add g16_age_ms (runtime, default 0). When set, a ready job that has
waited that many ms, since it was queued or its queue was last served,
is picked ahead of every job that has not, whatever the priorities; of
two such jobs the one that waited longer goes first. While no job has
waited that long, the order is the three keys above. The stats file
counts the jobs published after they aged. The order itself is in
age.rs, a core module with host tests.

Each job and queue now records when it was queued or served: two clock
reads per job, whatever the word.

Assisted-by: LLM
